description:
repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/docs-align-scan.ts checks timelessness, naming_alignment, accuracy, setup_enablement and shape_existence, but nothing checks a doc sentence that asserts a RUNTIME BEHAVIOUR. Concretely, docs/FOUNDATION_COMPLIANCE_CHECKS.md contains "The closure-audit script runs CC-001..CC-007 nightly and emits a `closureStatusReport` impulse." That sentence is false — closureStatusReport is advertised by no vessel and the script has no caller — and no invariant can see it. The `accuracy` invariant cannot: it requires the word "shape" or "resolver" on the line (this line says "impulse") and then a declaration form (`type: "X"` or `shape: "X"`), neither of which a prose behavioural claim has. That narrowing is deliberate and must not be widened.

Add a new, deliberately narrow `behavioral_claim` invariant. Apply the two literal edits below; both anchors occur exactly once.

EDIT 1 — replace this exact block:

  const invariants = new Set(pointer.invariants ?? [
    "timelessness",
    "naming_alignment",
    "accuracy",
    "setup_enablement",
    "shape_existence",
  ]);

with this exact block:

  const invariants = new Set(pointer.invariants ?? [
    "timelessness",
    "naming_alignment",
    "accuracy",
    "setup_enablement",
    "shape_existence",
    "behavioral_claim",
  ]);

EDIT 2 — insert this exact block IMMEDIATELY BEFORE this line:

    if (invariants.has("shape_existence") && vocabulary) {

the block to insert:

    // behavioral_claim: a doc sentence that asserts the system EMITS a named impulse is a
    // promise about runtime behaviour, and it is checkable — the shape is either advertised or
    // it is not. This is the class `accuracy` deliberately cannot reach: that check requires a
    // DECLARATION form (type:/shape:) on a line mentioning "shape" or "resolver", and a prose
    // claim like "emits a `closureStatusReport` impulse" has neither.
    //
    // Kept narrow on purpose, for the reason the accuracy block above already records: a
    // validator that fires on correct docs trains its readers to ignore it. Both the emission
    // verb AND the word "impulse" must be present on the line, so ordinary prose that merely
    // names a shape does not qualify.
    if (invariants.has("behavioral_claim") && pointer.live_truth && typeof pointer.live_truth !== "string" && pointer.live_truth.advertised_shapes) {
      const advertisedClaims = new Set(pointer.live_truth.advertised_shapes);
      const emissionRx = /\b(?:emits?|emitting|emitted|publishes|writes)\b/i;
      for (const line of lines) {
        if (!emissionRx.test(line)) continue;
        if (!/\bimpulse\b/i.test(line)) continue;
        if (/\bexamples?\b|\be\.g\.|\bfor instance\b|\bhypothetical\b|\bviolation:/i.test(line)) continue;
        const claimRx = /`([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]*)`/g;
        let cm: RegExpExecArray | null;
        while ((cm = claimRx.exec(line)) !== null) {
          const claimed = cm[1];
          if (!claimed) continue;
          if (!/^[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9_]*$/.test(claimed)) continue;
          if (advertisedClaims.has(claimed)) continue;
          if (!pushFinding({
            doc_id: doc.id,
            invariant: "behavioral_claim",
            evidence: line,
            suggested_repair: `the doc claims this emits "${claimed}", but no vessel advertises that shape; verify the behaviour exists or remove the claim`,
          })) break outer;
        }
      }
    }

Change nothing else. Do not touch the accuracy, shape_existence, timelessness, naming_alignment or setup_enablement blocks. The new block sits inside the same `outer:` labelled loop over `documents`, so `doc`, `lines`, `pushFinding` and `break outer` are all in scope exactly as the neighbouring blocks use them.

The file must typecheck, and test/resolvers/docs-align-scan.test.ts must keep passing — the new invariant only ADDS findings for lines carrying both an emission verb and the word "impulse", so existing expectations are unaffected unless a fixture already contains such a line.

